1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:03,465
This is the document Priya actually handed Marcus.

2
00:00:03,465 --> 00:00:04,836
Open the C10 template.

3
00:00:04,836 --> 00:00:09,571
Six short sections: what this is, the numbers from C09, the safeguards named

4
00:00:09,571 --> 00:00:14,617
plainly, what you are asking for, what you are explicitly not asking for, and, if

5
00:00:14,617 --> 00:00:17,919
colleagues are involved, what they commit to as well.

6
00:00:17,919 --> 00:00:22,343
Read the six as one arc and you can hear the whole course in miniature.

7
00:00:22,343 --> 00:00:26,081
What this is: the job description, compressed to a sentence.

8
00:00:26,081 --> 00:00:28,448
The numbers: Section 9, counted clean.

9
00:00:28,448 --> 00:00:31,626
The safeguards: Sections 6 and 8, in plain clothes.

10
00:00:31,626 --> 00:00:35,488
The ask and the not-ask: Section 9.4's limits, turned outward.

11
00:00:35,488 --> 00:00:38,853
The commitments: Section 7's trials, made contractual.

12
00:00:38,853 --> 00:00:44,024
Nothing on the page is new, which is why filling it in takes twenty minutes and not

13
00:00:44,024 --> 00:00:48,385
a weekend: you are transcribing work you already did, into the order a

14
00:00:48,385 --> 00:00:49,880
decision-maker reads in.

15
00:00:50,430 --> 00:00:55,109
Naming the boundary is what turns a request into something a careful manager can

16
00:00:55,109 --> 00:00:59,847
approve quickly, because it closes off the worry they would otherwise have to ask

17
00:00:59,847 --> 00:01:00,841
about themselves.

18
00:01:00,841 --> 00:01:05,755
You already know why this matters: it is Section 9.4's never-automate list, restated

19
00:01:05,755 --> 00:01:07,100
for an audience of one.

20
00:01:07,100 --> 00:01:11,194
The section works because approvals are really priced on the downside.

21
00:01:11,194 --> 00:01:15,874
Every manager reading a request is privately extending it: if I say yes to this,

22
00:01:15,874 --> 00:01:17,570
what am I saying yes to next?

23
00:01:17,570 --> 00:01:22,307
The not-asking line answers that unasked question in writing, which collapses the

24
00:01:22,307 --> 00:01:24,881
imagined slope back into the actual request.

25
00:01:24,881 --> 00:01:29,794
Write yours for your own job: not asking to change who approves invoices, not asking

26
00:01:29,794 --> 00:01:34,357
to let anything send without review, whatever the escalation your reader would

27
00:01:34,357 --> 00:01:35,175
silently fear.

28
00:01:35,175 --> 00:01:40,089
If you cannot think of one, you have not imagined being your manager for long enough

29
00:01:40,089 --> 00:01:40,323
yet.

30
00:01:40,873 --> 00:01:45,748
That first line matters more than it looks, and the next lecture tells you exactly

31
00:01:45,748 --> 00:01:50,445
why: what happened the first week someone other than Priya actually used one of

32
00:01:50,445 --> 00:01:51,396
these templates.

33
00:01:51,396 --> 00:01:55,023
Resist the urge to soften it for colleagues who seem capable.

34
00:01:55,023 --> 00:01:57,995
Capability was never the variable the trial tests.

35
00:01:57,995 --> 00:02:02,633
Their world is, and nobody's capability has ever made their world match yours.

36
00:02:03,183 --> 00:02:05,872
The "this week" is not motivational garnish.

37
00:02:05,872 --> 00:02:10,578
Your C09 numbers are at their most defensible right now, while the weeks they

38
00:02:10,578 --> 00:02:15,651
describe are still recent enough to answer questions about, and your own command of

39
00:02:15,651 --> 00:02:19,502
the material fades faster than you expect once the course ends.

40
00:02:19,502 --> 00:02:24,330
A one-pager delivered this week is a status update from someone on top of their

41
00:02:24,330 --> 00:02:24,636
work.

42
00:02:24,636 --> 00:02:28,914
The same page in three months is a proposal you have to warm up again.

43
00:02:28,914 --> 00:02:33,987
You now have every artifact this course builds toward: ten documents, on your desk.

44
00:02:33,987 --> 00:02:38,633
Next lecture tells you what happened the first time someone else opened one.
